# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_DEBUGLOG_CREATE */
int64_t debuglog_create(uint32_t resource, uint32_t options, uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_DEBUGLOG_WRITE */
int64_t debuglog_write(uint32_t log, uint32_t options, const void *text,
                       uint64_t length);
```

# DESCRIPTION

The debuglog is a ring of 512 records of 256 bytes (`CROI_LOG_RING_SLOTS`, `CROI_LOG_RECORD_MAX`), the oldest overwritten when it is full.
The kernel and every process write to it.
A record is a `croi_log_record_t` followed by its text: a sequence number, the length, the severity, flags, a monotonic timestamp in nanoseconds, and the koids of the writing process and thread (0 for the kernel).
A kernel thread copies each record to the console as `[sssss.mmm] pid:tid> text`, so a writer never waits for the UART.

Readers do not call the kernel.
The ring is a VMO that a process maps read only, with the ring format `CROI_LOG_RING_FORMAT`, and a counter holding the number of records written, which a caught-up reader binds with `CROI_BIND_VALUE` at its cursor plus one (port(2), counter(2)).
Both are in the startup handles of a process that is given them.
object_get_info(2) `CROI_INFO_DEBUGLOG` on a log handle reports how many records have been written and how many the console has finished with, for writers that pace themselves to the console.

`debuglog_create` writes to <out> a handle to a log.
<resource> and <options> must both be 0 (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`): a log can only write, so it needs no authority.
The handle has the basic rights, WRITE and SIGNAL.

`debuglog_write` appends a record of <length> bytes of <text> to the log.
The handle needs WRITE.
<options> is 0 or `CROI_LOG_LOCAL`, which is stored in the record's flags; anything else is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
Text longer than `CROI_LOG_RECORD_DATA_MAX` (216) bytes is cut to that length without an error.
The severity of a record from a process is `CROI_LOG_INFO`.
The call returns 0.

